# Carrier Wound #21: btoa() Latin1 Ceiling Forges a Planner Outage

The very first dream-shape run on freshly-deployed wound-#20 code blocked
terminal in `planningDynamicWorkflow`, before any node executed:

```json
"blocker": {
  "code": "adapter_unavailable",
  "message": "Dynamic workflow planner failed: btoa() can only operate on characters in the Latin1 (ISO/IEC 8859-1) range."
}
```

`driveGeneration 1`, `checkpoint null`, no node attempts, `hasRunStartRecord
false`. A healthy planner agent never got to run — the carrier killed it while
**packing the command**, then surfaced the catch-all `adapter_unavailable`. This
is exactly the failure the "observe the run live, don't stare and guess" mandate
exists to catch: the wound was invisible from the outside and obvious node-by-node.

## Mechanism

The Worker ships every Sandbox command by base64-encoding the bash script,
piping it through `base64 -d` on the container, and running the decoded bytes:

```ts
const wrapCommandForSandbox = (command: string): string => {
  const encodedCommand = btoa(command); // ← wound
  return String.raw`...printf '%s' '${encodedCommand}' | base64 -d > "$script_path"...`;
};
```

In the Workers runtime, `btoa()` only accepts code points in the Latin1
(ISO/IEC 8859-1) range — it throws `InvalidCharacterError` on any character
above `0xFF`. The **Dynamic workflow planner is itself an agent lane**, and its
command embeds the source material it plans over. Dream transcripts carry emoji
(🌙), smart quotes (" "), em-dashes (–), accented Latin (café), CJK (日本語) —
all above `0xFF`. So `btoa` detonated the instant real content flowed through.
The polite test corpus had been pure ASCII; production was not. A franchise of
the [[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]: **the doubles were politer than the
transcripts.**

The bug is a category error — conflating a string's **code points** with its
**byte encoding**:

> "you can't spend four bytes encoding a code point that would fit in three.
> This rule ensures that there is exactly one UTF-8 encoding for a given code
> point." — Programming Rust, 2nd Ed. (Blandy & Orendorff), Ch. 17

`btoa` wants bytes (0–255). UTF-8 text has code points far above that. You must
encode the **UTF-8 bytes**, not hand `btoa` the raw code points.

## Fix — Encode the Bytes, as a Class

New module `src/app/infrastructure/sandbox-command-encoding.ts` owns the one
Worker-side base64 chokepoint:

```ts
export const encodeCommandToBase64 = (command: string): string => {
  const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode(command);
  const chunkSize = 32_768;
  let binary = "";
  for (let offset = 0; offset < bytes.length; offset += chunkSize) {
    binary += String.fromCodePoint(...bytes.subarray(offset, offset + chunkSize));
  }
  return btoa(binary);
};
```

Three properties matter:

1. **Bytes, not code points.** `TextEncoder().encode` yields the UTF-8 byte
   sequence; each byte (0–255) maps to one Latin1 character, so `btoa` never sees
   a value it can't handle.
2. **Decode path is unchanged.** `base64 -d` decodes back to those exact UTF-8
   bytes → file → bash reads the original UTF-8 script. No container-side change.
3. **Chunked.** Bytes feed `fromCodePoint` in 32 KiB windows so multi-megabyte
   transcripts can't overflow the argument stack — verified with ~1 MB of emoji.

`wrapCommandForSandbox` now calls `encodeCommandToBase64`. It is the **only**
`btoa` in `src/`, so fixing this chokepoint fixes the whole class. (The
container-side result marker already uses node `Buffer.from(...).toString(
"base64")`, which is byte-correct.)

## Chaos Receipt (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/sandbox-command-encoding.test.ts` — 5 passing. The hostile
double is production's **real decode transport**: pipe the encoded payload
through bash `base64 -d` (`execFileSync`), not a polite JS `atob`. A JS-only
round-trip would have masked the byte-vs-code-point bug; `base64 -d` does not.

- raw `btoa(nonLatin1)` throws `/character/iu`; `encodeCommandToBase64` does not
- non-Latin1 (emoji + smart quotes + em-dash + CJK + Cyrillic) round-trips
  through bash `base64 -d` byte-for-byte
- plain ASCII round-trips unchanged
- ~1 MB of 4-byte code points round-trips without stack overflow
- output is standard padded base64 (`/^[A-Za-z0-9+/]+={0,2}$/`)

## Verification

```bash
pnpm typecheck   # clean
pnpm check       # oxfmt + oxlint + type-aware, clean
pnpm vitest run tests/integration/sandbox-command-encoding.test.ts \
  tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts   # 5 + 21 pass
```

## Burn-In Gate

Per the Phase 2 D3 acceptance bar, the 10-run count stays at **zero**. This fix
must be deployed and a fresh dream-shape run must reach `captured` **observed
live on deployed code** before the burn-in restarts. Any failure is a carrier
failure: fix the class, add the chaos test that would have caught it, reset the
count. Wound #21 is the second class found this way after [[carrier-wound-json-lane-lossy-text-2026-06-14]];
both surfaced only because the run was watched node-by-node, not stared at.
